  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the smaller exponential as one variable",
      "body": "<p>Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Exponential terms differing by a squared base or a fixed exponent shift can share one substitution.",
      "pitfall": "The fixed factor is 9, not 4 or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Write the equation as a quadratic in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "Exponential terms differing by a squared base or a fixed exponent shift can share one substitution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Return from the exponential variable",
      "body": "Solve your quadratic and keep only roots in the allowed range of the exponential. Apply logarithms to recover the original inputs.",
      "recap": "When adding logarithms of the same base, multiply their positive arguments.",
      "pitfall": "A root of the substituted quadratic is not yet a root in the original variable.",
      "pause": "Recover every real input and combine their sum using a logarithm law.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l06",
          "reminder": "When adding logarithms of the same base, multiply their positive arguments."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The equation becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, whose roots are 4 and 5. Both are admissible.</p><p>Taking base-3 logarithms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> or 5 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. Their sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer E.</strong></p>",
